        "Consider a bank that funds loans with insured retail deposits, uninsured wholesale funding and equity. When the central bank raises the policy rate, the opportunity cost of holding deposits rises, deposits flow out or become more expensive [14], and the bank must either replace them with wholesale funding, run down liquid assets or reduce lending. The cost of wholesale funding depends on the bank's perceived solvency: a well-capitalised bank can raise uninsured funding at a small premium, whereas a poorly capitalised bank faces a higher premium or is rationed [5][25]. Similarly, a bank with a large buffer of liquid assets can absorb a deposit outflow by selling securities rather than cutting loans [4]. Capital also matters directly: a bank close to its regulatory minimum may need to shrink its assets if tightening reduces its profits or raises loan-loss provisions [7].",
